window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][77] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How should a platform owner respond if regulation opens alternative app distribution?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Fight it,&quot; or &quot;comply and accept lower revenue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what is at stake (revenue, security, quality, developers, users) and lays out response options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Models revenue under different shifts, separates what regulation requires from what the owner chooses, considers second-order effects on security and developers, and proposes a staged response with measures.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is an Illustrative scenario, with regulatory facts as of 2026-10 <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026, on US and EU proceedings; the EU Digital Markets Act]</code>; I know the area only from public information, and nothing here describes any real company's plans. Several jurisdictions have adopted or considered rules that require large platforms to allow other app stores or payment methods. I'd treat the question as how to respond, in order. <em>Diagnosis.</em> What is at stake: (1) <em>revenue</em>: the platform earns a commission on developer billings; (2) <em>security and quality</em>: the platform's review and distribution control is part of its promise to users; (3) <em>developers</em>: they want lower fees and direct relationships; (4) <em>users</em>: they value safety, simplicity and choice; (5) <em>the ecosystem advantage</em>: integration between devices and services (C18.5). <em>Revenue under shifts (illustrative index).</em> Developers bill 100 through the platform; commission 30%: revenue 30. Suppose regulation allows alternatives, and the platform charges a reduced technology fee of 5% on billings that move. If 10% of billings move: 90 × 30% + 10 × 5% = 27 + 0.5 = 27.5, down 8%. If 30% move: 70 × 30% + 30 × 5% = 21 + 1.5 = 22.5, down 25%. The size of the shift is the swing assumption, and it depends on how much developers gain by moving and how much users will follow. <em>Options.</em> (A) Contest the rule and comply minimally. (B) Comply fully and compete on value: invest in the quality of the platform's own store and services so that users and developers choose it. (C) Comply and redesign the commercial terms (a lower commission with a fee for services such as payments, review, discovery, and updates) so that the platform's revenue tracks the value it provides. (D) Comply with strong safeguards (verification, notarization, clear warnings) for alternative channels. <em>Evaluation.</em> A delays and risks penalties and goodwill. B and C aim to keep users and developers on the platform by merit. D protects users from the new risks (malware, scams) that alternative channels can create, a real second-order effect. The options combine: C and D are compatible, and B is the long-term goal. <em>Second-order effects.</em> Security incidents on alternative channels could harm users and the platform's reputation even if the platform is not at fault; developers may fragment their releases; competitors may exploit the opening; privacy promises may be harder to keep. Regulators may scrutinize the new terms themselves. <em>Recommendation.</em> Comply in good faith, restructure commercial terms to charge for value (C), build the safeguards (D), and invest in making the platform's store the best place to distribute (B). Measure: share of billings that move, security incident rate, developer satisfaction, user trust, and revenue per user. Stage by region where rules apply. I'd change my mind if the share of billings moving were small, which would mean less urgency, or large, which would call for a deeper rethink of the model. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only (a platform owner of the kind Apple and Google are): <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that strategy prompts at Apple include platform and regulatory scenarios <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any company's actual response, fees or rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An enterprise software company has three business lines: a mature suite that funds the company, a fast-growing platform, and an experimental line. How do you allocate investment?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Put the most money in the growing business.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Assigns each line a role and criteria, and allocates with reasons.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Protects the cash source, sets stage gates for the experiment, uses numbers on return, names interdependencies between lines, and states what would change the allocation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know such companies only from public information. The first move is to give each line a role, because the right investment depends on what the line is for. <em>Roles (invented figures).</em> The <em>mature suite</em>: revenue 60 (index), growth 3% a year, margin 45%. Its role is to fund the company and to defend against erosion. The <em>growth platform</em>: revenue 30, growth 25%, margin 30%. Its role is to become the next major business and to be the company's future. The <em>experimental line</em>: revenue 2, growth 100%, margin deeply negative. Its role is to find out whether a new bet is worth scaling. <em>Criteria.</em> Return on the strategic goal, size of the opportunity, strength of the advantage, risk, and interdependence (does one line strengthen another?). For example, the platform may sell to the suite's customers, and the suite's relationships may be its distribution. <em>Allocation logic.</em> Suppose 10 units of investment capacity. <em>Suite:</em> enough to defend and modernize, since it produces 27 of profit (60 × 45%) and any erosion hurts everything; say 2. <em>Platform:</em> the largest share, since growth at 25% on 30 is 7.5 of new revenue a year, with a good chance of becoming the largest line; say 6. <em>Experiment:</em> a bounded amount with stage gates, say 2, released in stages against milestones (customer evidence, unit economics) with a stop rule. <em>Numbers to check.</em> The platform's growth adds 7.5 in revenue at 30% margin, about 2.25 of profit a year at current margins, rising as scale grows; the experiment may have a low chance of success but a high payoff. The suite's decline rate matters: if it fell by 5 a year, that would be worth more than a small platform gain, so I'd check what the suite needs to hold its position. <em>Interdependencies.</em> If the platform's customers come through the suite's relationships, cutting suite investment harms the platform. I'd map such dependencies. <em>Decision and review.</em> Allocate about 2, 6 and 2, review each quarter, and shift if the platform's growth slows, if the suite shows faster erosion or if the experiment reaches a gate. What would change my mind: evidence that the experiment is more promising than its stage suggests (a larger share), or that the suite's customers are at risk of leaving (more to defend). <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that portfolio-allocation prompts at Microsoft involve mature, growth and experimental lines <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or plan is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A large online marketplace is considering selling its own branded products in categories where third-party sellers do well. Should it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, own brands have higher margins,&quot; or &quot;no, it will upset sellers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs margin against seller trust and regulatory risk, with numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Models the net including diverted commission and trust effects, identifies the mechanism of the marketplace advantage (selection, trust), proposes safeguards, and states a test and conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know the topic only from public information, and nothing here describes any real company's practice. Marketplaces and their owners' own products have been the subject of public scrutiny and regulatory attention in several jurisdictions <code>[V: European Commission decision of 20 December 2022 on Amazon's use of seller data and Buy Box; Digital Markets Act self-preferencing rule]</code>, which is part of the risk. <em>Diagnosis.</em> A marketplace's advantage is selection and trust: buyers come because it has what they want and they can rely on the process; sellers come because buyers are there (a network effect, C18.5). The question is whether competing with sellers erodes that advantage. <em>Numbers (illustrative).</em> A category has gross merchandise value of $1 billion. The marketplace earns a 15% commission: $150 million. Suppose its own brand captures 10% of the category ($100 million of sales) at a 35% margin: $35 million. But those sales come largely from sellers' sales, so the commission on them is lost: 15% × $100 million = $15 million. Net: +$20 million. Now suppose sellers respond: a few leave and others raise prices, and the category's total sales fall 3% ($30 million), costing $4.5 million of commission. Net: +$15.5 million. If the response is worse (a 10% fall, $100 million, $15 million of commission), the net is +$5 million and the long-run damage could exceed it. The estimate of the seller and buyer response is the swing assumption. <em>Beyond the arithmetic.</em> Trust has a multiplier: sellers make decisions about where to invest based on whether the platform will use their success against them. Concerns include use of sellers' sales data to choose what to copy, favored placement for own products, and the effect on buyer trust if results look biased. Regulation can change the economics overnight. <em>Options.</em> (A) No own brands. (B) Own brands only where sellers are thin (gaps in selection), which fills selection and competes less directly. (C) Own brands with strict safeguards: no use of individual sellers' data, clear labeling, no preferential ranking, and independent audit. (D) Free entry in all categories. <em>Recommendation.</em> B with C's safeguards: enter where selection or quality is poor, which expands the marketplace's value, and keep firm rules on data and ranking. Measure: seller retention and investment, buyer trust, category growth, and the diverted-commission arithmetic, with a stop rule if seller exits exceed a threshold. What would change my mind: evidence that sellers in the category are leaving or that regulators are acting, which would favor A. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only (a large marketplace operator): <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that marketplace-conflict prompts appear at Amazon <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about Amazon's actual practices, data or decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your productivity-software company wants to add voice transcription to its notes product. Should it build the capability, buy a company that has it, or partner with a provider?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it, because we should own our technology,&quot; or &quot;partner, because it's faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares the options on cost, time and control, and says whether the capability is core.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Decides by whether the capability differentiates the product, models the volume at which build overtakes partner, prices the delay as well as the spend, protects against dependence (data, terms, exit), and says what would change the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Start with the question that decides the rest (the chooser is FW-C18-07).</em> Is transcription something customers choose us for, or something they expect as a basic part of notes? If it is a basic expectation, speed and cost matter most and ownership matters least; if it is where we want to win (for example, a unique accuracy on meetings), control and learning matter more. <em>Options and figures (all invented).</em> Partner: license a provider at $0.012 a minute with about six weeks to integrate. Build: about $3.0 million and twelve months, with a run cost of $0.004 a minute. Buy: a company at about $25 million with a team of twenty engineers and a small user base. At 10 million minutes a month, partner costs 0.012 × 10,000,000 × 12 = $1.44 million a year and build costs $0.48 million a year to run, so build's extra $3.0 million is repaid in 3.0 ÷ (1.44 − 0.48) = about 3.1 years. At 30 million minutes a month, partner costs $4.32 million a year against $1.44 million, and build is repaid in about one year. So volume decides when building pays. <em>Delay has a price.</em> Building takes twelve months against six weeks: if the feature is expected to lift retention or win deals, those twelve months of lost benefit belong in the comparison, as does the risk that a competitor ships first. <em>Risks of each.</em> Partner: dependence on a provider's price and quality, and where our customers' audio goes (privacy and data terms). Build: delivery risk and distraction from the roadmap. Buy: integration risk and paying for a team and product we may not need; it makes sense only when the capability and the team are both scarce and strategic. <em>Recommendation.</em> Partner now, to ship in weeks and learn how customers use it, with contract terms that protect data, cap price increases and allow an exit; revisit building when volume passes about 25 million minutes a month or when accuracy for our use case becomes a reason customers choose us. Buy only if a partner cannot meet the quality bar and a team with the capability is available at a price that is small beside the value. I'd measure adoption, accuracy on our own audio, cost per minute and customer privacy concerns, and I'd change my view if the provider's terms worsened or our volume grew faster than expected. <em>Signals.</em> The core-or-context question; a computed break-even on volume; the price of delay; dependence risks; a staged recommendation with triggers.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What should the product vision be for a mid-sized photo-sharing app over the next three years, and how would you turn it into a roadmap?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A list of features: &quot;AI editing, video, a marketplace.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States who the product is for, the change it makes in their lives, and a few strategic bets, ordered in time.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Grounds the vision in user and market trends and the company's advantages, states what it will not do, converts it into outcomes and bets by horizon with metrics and kill criteria, and says how it would learn that the vision is wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Ground it.</em> A vision should answer who we serve, what changes for them, and why we can win. I'd start from evidence: how people share photos now (messaging apps and private groups, more than public feeds), what our users do that is distinctive (suppose families sharing within small circles), and what we do better than large platforms (privacy, a calm experience, long-term storage and organization). I'd also name the trends that could hurt us: sharing moving to messaging, and phone makers building photo features into the device. <em>A vision statement.</em> &quot;The place where a family's photos live and are shared with the people who matter, for decades.&quot; It is specific about the user (families and close circles), the job (keep and share), and the quality (private and lasting). It leaves out public fame-seeking and ad-driven feeds, which is a strategic choice: what we will not do. <em>From vision to outcomes by horizon.</em> Now (next two quarters): make sharing within circles effortless, measured by shares per active family and the share of new users who invite a second person within a week (say from 28% to 40%). Next (quarters three to six): make the archive worth keeping, through search by person and event and annual books, measured by weekly retrievals per user and 12-month retention. Later (year two and three): extend to shared family history and relatives who are not users, measured by non-user recipients who become users and by paid conversion. For each bet I'd write the assumption, a small test and a kill criterion (C26), so that the roadmap is a set of testable bets, not promised features. <em>Guardrails and honesty.</em> The roadmap should commit to outcomes and ranges, and show confidence falling with distance: high for now, medium for next, low for later. I'd keep capacity for the unplanned and review the vision yearly against what the data says. <em>How I'd know it's wrong.</em> Leading indicators: if circles do not grow, if retrieval stays low, or if shares move to messaging apps faster than we can integrate. I'd set a review date and thresholds in advance, and be ready to change the target user, not just the features. <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-based user and trend grounding; a specific vision with exclusions; outcomes by horizon with metrics; bets with kill criteria; how it could be wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a platform? How is a two-sided market different from a developer platform?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A platform is a big app that lots of people use.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a platform by multiple participant groups and gives both types with examples.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the different core jobs and hard problems of each, notes hybrids, and says when the label does not apply.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A platform is a product that others build on or transact through, which creates value for more than one group. The defining feature is that the value to one group depends on another group's participation. A <em>two-sided market</em> has two groups who need each other: riders and drivers, buyers and sellers. The platform's core job is matching and trust. The hard problems are the cold start (neither side comes until the other is there), keeping enough supply near the demand (liquidity), and disintermediation (participants bypassing the platform after a match). The primary metric is successful transactions. A <em>developer platform</em> provides a foundation that others build products on, through APIs and tools: an operating system, a cloud service, a payments API. The core job is to be reliable, well-documented and stable. The hard problems are developer experience (how fast a developer succeeds), stability (not breaking what people built), and governance. The primary metric is developers who ship to production, or what they build. Many platforms are both: an app store has developers (who build) and users (who buy), so it faces review-policy, fee and discovery problems on top of the two kinds of problem. When it does not apply: a product with many users but no interaction among them and no outside builders is just a popular product. Calling it a platform imports the wrong questions. My first step with a platform question is to name the sides and what each gives and gets.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A freelance marketplace has 5,000 freelancers and 300 clients. Clients complain they cannot find the right person, and freelancers complain they get no work. What's going on, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Get more clients with marketing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Diagnoses a supply-demand imbalance and a matching problem, and proposes narrowing.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the ratio, uses liquidity, separates the two complaints, and proposes seeding demand and curating supply, with a metric.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The two complaints are one problem seen from each side. There are about 17 freelancers for each client (5,000 ÷ 300), so work is scarce for freelancers, and with so many to choose among and no structure, clients struggle to find the right person. The platform has too much unfocused supply and too little demand. <em>Diagnose by segment.</em> Not all freelancers compete for the same work. Suppose most are in a few popular categories while demand is concentrated in a few others (invented). Look at the match rate and time to hire by category: some categories may be healthy, others empty on one side. <em>What I'd do.</em> (1) <em>Narrow:</em> focus on the categories where demand exists and where the platform can reach density, and stop recruiting supply in saturated ones. (2) <em>Raise the quality of matching for clients:</em> better search, a short brief that routes to a small set of qualified candidates, so clients see a few strong options and not a thousand. (3) <em>Grow demand where supply is strong:</em> outbound to clients who need those skills. (4) <em>Curate supply:</em> verified skills and reviews, so clients can trust what they see and the best freelancers get the work, which keeps them. <em>Metric.</em> Not sign-ups. Primary: the share of client briefs that result in a hire within a week (hire rate), with time to first qualified proposal, and freelancer earnings for the top and median freelancer as guardrails (if only a handful earn, supply will leave). I'd check that clients rehire. <em>Risk.</em> Cutting supply feels like shrinking; the aim is balance. What would change my mind: if clients' real issue is trust and not selection, in which case verification and protection matter more than matching.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A payments API team can ship a cleaner design that simplifies integration for new developers but breaks 3% of existing integrations. Do you ship it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, simpler is better,&quot; or &quot;No, never break developers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs benefits for new developers against costs to existing ones, and considers versioning.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies both, proposes a non-breaking alternative (a new version alongside the old), a deprecation timeline and migration support, and treats trust as an asset.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general: I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. I'd not ship it as a breaking change on a flag day. I'd look for a way to get the benefit without the break. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> 200,000 businesses have integrated; 3% is 6,000. Helping each migrate costs about $150 of support and engineering time: $900,000 once. The benefit: a simpler API raises the share of new developers who reach production from 56% to 62% (a better first mile, C19.3); with 20,000 new developers a year, that is 1,200 more integrations, each worth $2,000 a year in revenue (invented): $2.4 million a year. The risk: if 5% of the 6,000 affected businesses leave because of the disruption, 300 integrations are lost at $2,000: $600,000 a year. Net in year one: $2.4 million − $0.9 million − $0.6 million = +$0.9 million. That looks positive, but it ignores trust: a platform known for breaking integrations raises the cost of building on it for everyone, and that is hard to measure and costly to lose. <em>Alternative.</em> Ship the cleaner design as a new version, and keep the old working. New developers get the simple path; existing integrations are untouched. Cost: maintaining two versions for a while. Then deprecate the old version on a published schedule (say 24 months), with notice, a migration guide and tooling, and focus support on the businesses most affected. This captures most of the $2.4 million without the $0.6 million risk and the trust cost. <em>Decision.</em> Introduce the new version, keep the old, announce a long deprecation path, and measure adoption of the new version by new and existing developers, support tickets, and churn among those who migrate. What would change my mind: if the old design carries a security flaw, a shorter timeline with strong support is justified. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of developer-platform prompts and <code>Public</code> that Stripe documents API versioning <code>[V: Stripe API versioning documentation; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No Stripe data or plan is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A software vendor accounts for 12% of your enterprise marketplace's revenue. It asks for a lower revenue share and a say in your roadmap. How do you respond?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Give it what it wants, it's too big to lose,&quot; or &quot;treat everyone the same.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the vendor's value and leverage against fairness to other vendors, and offers structured options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the cost, separates the economic request from the influence request, proposes volume tiers open to all and a partner council, and addresses concentration risk and the precedent.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general: I know such platforms only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. Two requests are bundled and need separating: price and influence. <em>Price.</em> Suppose the marketplace's revenue is $50 million at a 20% share, so the vendor generates $6 million in revenue, on $30 million of sales. A cut to 12% would reduce revenue from that vendor to $3.6 million, a loss of $2.4 million a year. If other vendors learn of a special deal, they will ask for it, and trust in the rules erodes. If the vendor really generates 12% of revenue, it is also a concentration risk: its departure would hurt, which is a reason to reduce dependence as well as to keep it. <em>Principled answer.</em> Offer a published volume tier that any vendor can qualify for, with a lower rate above a sales threshold, perhaps with a requirement that gives the platform something in return (co-selling, deeper integration, support commitments). The vendor gets a better rate because of its size, and every other vendor can see the rule and aim for it. <em>Influence.</em> A seat on a partner advisory council, where several vendors give input to the roadmap, is reasonable; a private veto is not, because it would trade the platform's neutrality and its other vendors' trust for one account. I'd listen to the vendor's needs (the real product problems) and consider them on the same criteria as other requests. <em>Concentration.</em> Work on the broader base: grow the number of mid-sized vendors and measure the share of revenue from the top 10 (target a decline over time). <em>Decision.</em> Offer the volume tier and the council, hold on a bespoke deal, and give the vendor a dated plan for roadmap items that serve many vendors. What would change my mind: if the vendor is truly strategic (it brings customers that others do not), a bigger commitment from them in exchange for a deeper partnership, structured as a tier open to peers. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of enterprise partner and marketplace prompts at Microsoft <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or program is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the review policy for an app store: speed versus safety versus developer trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Review everything carefully,&quot; or &quot;make it fast.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the three goals and designs a tiered process.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sorts submissions by risk, quantifies reviewer capacity, builds explanations and appeals, measures false rejections and harmful approvals, and sets governance for changes to the rules.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the topic only from public information; this describes a design for a store of the kind Apple and Google operate, not any real process. The three goals pull against each other (FW-C19-08). Strict review raises safety and costs speed and trust; light review raises speed and risk. The way through is to match scrutiny to risk. <em>Tier by risk.</em> Suppose 100,000 submissions a week (invented). Low risk (about 70%): updates to established apps with no new permissions or content types. Medium (25%): new apps in ordinary categories and updates that add features. High (5%): new apps in sensitive areas (payments, children, health, financial products), apps requesting powerful permissions, and apps from new accounts with signals of abuse. <em>Process.</em> Automated checks on everything (malware, policy violations, privacy declarations): minutes. Low risk: automated approval within an hour. Medium: automated checks plus a short human review, within 24 hours. High: deeper human review, two to three days, with specialists. <em>Capacity check.</em> Human time: 25,000 medium submissions at 0.5 hours is 12,500 hours; 5,000 high at 3 hours is 15,000 hours; total 27,500 hours a week, about 786 reviewers at 35 productive hours each. That is the real cost, and why automation and good tiering matter: each point of submissions moved from medium to low saves hundreds of reviewer hours. <em>Developer trust.</em> Published, specific rules with examples; consistent decisions (audit a sample for consistency); a reason with every rejection, tied to a rule; a fast appeals path with a different reviewer; a way to ask for guidance before submitting; predictable timing. Trust is lost mainly through opaque, inconsistent or slow decisions. <em>Safety.</em> Post-release monitoring (reports, anomaly detection), fast takedown and a way to revoke developer access; stronger identity checks for developers who handle sensitive data. <em>Measures.</em> Median and 95th-percentile time to decision by tier; harmful-approval rate (apps approved that later violate rules); false-rejection rate (rejections overturned on appeal), target under 1% of decisions; developer satisfaction; and user-reported harm. Guard against gaming: a faster median hides a long tail. <em>Governance of the rules.</em> Changes announced with notice, a comment period for significant changes, and a review of rules against outcomes each quarter. <em>Decision.</em> Tiered review with automation for the bulk, human depth where risk is high, explanations and appeals for trust, and the measures above. What would change my mind: a spike in harmful approvals (tighten the low tier) or a rise in overturned rejections (retrain reviewers or simplify rules). Regulatory requirements on app review differ by place and are changing <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026; EU Digital Markets Act]</code>. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes review guidelines and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that platform prompts at Apple include review policy <code>[V: Apple App Review Guidelines on Apple's developer site; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real review process, staffing or metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A home-services marketplace suspects that customers and providers are booking repeat jobs off the platform. How do you detect and reduce this?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ban contact information sharing&quot; or &quot;cut the fee.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines the problem, finds proxies to measure it, and offers value-based solutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the leakage, shows that a fee cut alone may not pay, designs continuing value, considers fairness to providers, and sets a test and guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Disintermediation is likely where the platform's value is the introduction and repeat jobs are easy to arrange directly. I'd start by measuring how big it is. <em>Detection.</em> Direct measurement is impossible, so use proxies: pairs of customer and provider with a first job and then no further bookings though the customer keeps using the category (the customer's other bookings continue with someone else, or provider availability shows busy); attempts to share contact details in messages; repeat-booking rates lower than in comparable categories; providers whose reviews mention working &quot;outside the app.&quot; Estimate leakage: suppose (invented) 100,000 first jobs a month, 40% of pairs would rebook, and 30% of those rebookings leak. With a 15% take rate on a $60 job ($9), leakage costs 100,000 × 0.4 × 0.3 × $9 = $108,000 a month. <em>Does cutting the fee on repeat jobs fix it?</em> Suppose repeat jobs are 40,000 a month; today 70% stay on-platform: 28,000 at $9 = $252,000. If a lower rate of 8% ($4.80) cut leakage from 30% to 10%, on-platform would be 36,000 at $4.80 = $172,800. Revenue falls. To keep revenue at $252,000 at $4.80 would need 52,500 repeat jobs on the platform, more than the 40,000 that exist. So a fee cut alone cannot pay; it must be paired with something else. <em>What works: continuing value.</em> Things the customer and provider cannot easily replicate off-platform: payment protection and dispute resolution, insurance or guarantees on damage, scheduling and reminders, recurring bookings with automatic payment, a verified record of work and reviews that the provider builds and takes pride in, financing or faster payouts for providers. If a guarantee costs $1 per job and cuts leakage from 30% to 20%, on-platform repeat jobs become 32,000 at ($9 − $1) = $256,000, which slightly exceeds today's $252,000 and builds trust. <em>Rules.</em> Contact-sharing bans have limited effect and annoy honest users; use them lightly for the first booking only, and focus on incentives. <em>Test and guardrails.</em> Pilot the guarantee and recurring-booking tools in two markets with a holdout; measure repeat rate on-platform, provider earnings, customer satisfaction and the proxy for leakage. What would change my mind: leakage proving far smaller than the proxies suggest, in which case the effort belongs elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Define the metrics you would use to run a developer platform for a cloud API, including how you would tell early that something is going wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of developers and API calls.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a primary metric and metrics for acquisition, activation, usage, quality and revenue.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses time to first call and production share, adds ecosystem health (concentration, churn), builds leading indicators and guardrails, and reads trends by cohort.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start from the platform's core value: developers building products that work and keep working. A volume metric such as API calls can rise while developer satisfaction and ecosystem health fall, so I'd use a primary metric and guardrails. <em>Primary metric.</em> Developers whose integrations are live in production and active over the last 30 days (production-active developers). It reflects success, not interest. <em>The funnel (FW-C19-04).</em> Sign-ups; the share who get credentials; the share who make a first successful call, with the median time to it; the share who reach production. Each stage is a place to look for friction; the first call time is the leading indicator of adoption. <em>Usage and value.</em> API calls per production-active developer; the number of distinct endpoints used (breadth); revenue per developer, and the share of developers growing their usage. <em>Quality and trust.</em> API availability and latency at the 95th and 99th percentiles; error rate for correct requests; support tickets per 1,000 developers and time to resolve; documentation feedback; the number of breaking-change incidents. <em>Ecosystem health.</em> Concentration: the share of API volume or revenue from the top 10 developers; developer churn (production-active developers lost per month) by cohort; multi-homing (developers also using rivals) from surveys; the share of new developers coming from referrals. <em>Early warnings.</em> (1) Time to first call lengthening for new cohorts, before sign-ups fall. (2) The production share of the latest sign-up cohort falling relative to earlier cohorts. (3) Rising error rates on a version before its deprecation. (4) Support ticket growth faster than developer growth. (5) Rising concentration, meaning growth is coming from a few large accounts. Read them by cohort, since averages blend old developers who are fine with new ones who are struggling (C13, C05). <em>Guardrails.</em> No improvement in sign-ups counts if the production share falls; no change counts that raises the error rate. <em>How I'd act.</em> A weekly dashboard by side and cohort, a threshold on each early warning with an owner, and a monthly review of developer interviews alongside the numbers. What would change the set: a platform with a very different developer type (enterprise integrators versus hobbyists) would need separate funnels.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a go-to-market plan, and what is a PM's role in it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the plan for launching a product with marketing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the elements: customer, value, positioning, price, channels, launch, metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes motions, assigns ownership across functions, ties the plan to a success measure, and gives an example.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A go-to-market plan is how a product reaches its target customers and wins them: who they are and what problem they have, what we say to them, what it costs and how it is packaged, how they find and buy it, how we launch, and how we'll know it's working. The motion varies: product-led for a consumer app or developer tool, sales-led for enterprise, partner-led where a platform brings the customers. &quot;The PM owns the customer problem, the value and the product, and works closely with product marketing on positioning and launch, with sales and support on enablement, and with finance on price. The PM usually co-owns the positioning and pricing decisions and carries the launch. For example, when we launched an expense-capture feature for small-business owners, I defined the target (owners tracking expenses in spreadsheets), the message (receipts captured automatically), the in-product launch and the success measure: the share of active owners who capture ten receipts in two weeks, with support contacts as a guardrail.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The elements; motions; ownership; a success measure.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Write a positioning statement for a new note-taking app for graduate students.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The best note-taking app for students.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the target, need, category, benefit and alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Targets by need, states a differentiator a competitor could not claim, supports it with proof, and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;For graduate students who read hundreds of papers and lose track of where an idea came from, [the app] is a notebook that links every note to its source passage. Unlike general note apps, where you copy and paste and lose the origin, it keeps the citation attached, so writing a literature review means assembling notes you can trace. &quot;What makes it sharp: the target is defined by a need (managing sources), not by 'students'; the benefit is one thing (traceable notes); the differentiator is specific; and a competitor could not say it unless they built it. Proof: a test with ten graduate students writing a short review, measuring time to assemble and citation errors against their current tools. For messaging I'd show three headlines to twenty students and ask which they'd repeat to a labmate and what they think the app does; I'd choose the one they can repeat and understand correctly.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Need-based target; one benefit; a differentiator; proof and a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Price a new premium wearable together with its services subscription.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd price it like competitors, maybe a bit higher because it's premium.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses cost floor, willingness to pay and the subscription's attach and margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the floor, finds the margin-maximizing price from willingness-to-pay data, models services economics over several years, tests a trial, and states the assumption that decides it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I reason from the structure of device-plus-services businesses and claim nothing about any real product or price. &quot;I'd set the price between a cost floor and the value ceiling and treat the services as a second profit stream. The unit cost is about $200, so a 55% margin gives a floor near $445. Competitors sit between $300 and $500, and the value is a daily health insight. From a willingness-to-pay survey, the margin per respondent is highest at $449 (about 102 against 92 at $349 and 77 at $549), so I'd start at $449 and test $399 and $499, since survey intent overstates purchase. &quot;For the subscription at $9.99 a month with a 70% margin, 35% attach and 80% annual renewal, services contribute about $72 per device over three years, which is about 29% on top of the $249 device margin. A three-month free trial that lifts attach to 50% costs about $7 per device to serve and wins by about $13 over three years, but only if attach reaches about 43%; below that it loses. So I'd test the trial on half of buyers and decide on 90-day attach and retention. &quot;Positioning: premium on craft and ecosystem value, not price alone; guardrails: returns, services churn and support load.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Floor, WTP-based price, services economics, the deciding assumption, a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you decide whether to add a new benefit to a paid membership program?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add it if customers want it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares cost per member with effects on retention and spend.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the break-even retention gain, accounts for selection and cannibalization, designs a holdout test with guardrails, and considers honest enrollment and cancellation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and no real program's economics <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;I'd compare the benefit's cost per member with its effect on the member's value. Suppose a member pays $120 a year and contributes $150 a year of margin on their spending, so a retained member-year is worth $270, and renewal today is 85%. The benefit costs $18 per member a year. If it lifts renewal by 1.5 points, that is worth 0.015 × $270 = $4.05; if it also raises spending by 2%, that adds $3.00; the total is $7.05 against a cost of $18, a net loss of about $11 per member. To break even the benefit would need to lift renewal by about 5.6 points ((18 − 3) ÷ 270), which is unlikely for one benefit. &quot;So I'd not add it as designed, and I'd ask what could change the math: a cheaper way to deliver it, or a benefit that targets a segment where the effect is bigger (members at risk of leaving). I'd test with a holdout, measure renewal and spending at 90 and 365 days, and watch for selection (heavy users take up the benefit most). I'd also check that the program remains honest: clear enrollment and easy cancellation, since regulators and customers care (C10.10).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Break-even computed; selection; a test; honesty about enrollment.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should an AI assistant be sold as an add-on to a productivity suite or bundled into the suite price?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Bundle it so everyone uses it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares revenue, cost and adoption for each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes contribution under each option, includes churn and cost to serve, considers strategic effects (adoption, data, competition), and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product or price <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;I'd compute the contribution of each option for 1,000 users. The suite is $12 per user per month; the assistant costs about $6 per user per month to serve for a full user and $2.40 on average if the average user is lighter. <em>Add-on:</em> priced at $20, 15% attach: revenue 1,000 × $12 + 150 × $20 = $15,000; cost 150 × $6 = $900; contribution $14,100. <em>Bundle:</em> the suite price rises to $17 for everyone; suppose 5% of users leave over the increase: 950 users at $17 is $16,150; cost 950 × $2.40 = $2,280; contribution $13,870. On these numbers the add-on is slightly better; if the price rise lost only 2% of users, the bundle would be about $14,300 and ahead. &quot;So the numbers are close, and the deciding factors are strategic: a bundle puts the assistant in front of everyone, which grows usage, feedback and habit, and may defend against competitors; an add-on protects margin and lets heavy users self-select, and reveals willingness to pay. I'd propose an add-on first, with a trial for all, then move to a bundle at a higher suite price if attach and usage show that most users value it. I'd test price and packaging on a sample of enterprise customers, with guardrails on renewal and complaints.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Computed comparison; sensitivity; strategic factors; a staged plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should a subscription streaming service launch a cheaper, ad-supported tier?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, to reach more subscribers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs new subscribers against cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes revenue under cannibalization assumptions, finds the break-even number of new subscribers, considers ad load and retention, and designs a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I reason from the structure of the decision and claim nothing about any real company's numbers <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;The question is whether the new tier adds more revenue than it takes from the premium tier. Suppose 1,000 premium subscribers at $15 a month: revenue $15,000. The ad tier is $8 plus about $5 a month of advertising revenue per subscriber, $13 in total. If 20% of premium subscribers move down and the tier adds 150 new subscribers, revenue is 800 × $15 + 200 × $13 + 150 × $13 = $16,550, up 10%. If 35% move down, it is 650 × $15 + 350 × $13 + 150 × $13 = $16,250. At 35% cannibalization, the tier needs at least 54 new subscribers to break even, about 5.4% growth. So the answer depends on the two uncertain numbers, the share who move down and the number who join. &quot;I'd also check ad revenue per subscriber, which depends on ad load and on advertisers, and retention: ad-tier subscribers may churn faster or slower; content costs are largely fixed, so the extra subscribers' margin is high. Fences matter: features the premium plan keeps (resolution, downloads, no ads) so that the move-down is limited. I'd test in one market with a holdout, measuring sign-ups, switching and retention at 90 days, with guardrails on satisfaction and ad complaints.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Computed cannibalization and break-even; ad economics; fences; a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your referral program has a viral coefficient of 0.4. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Try to get it above 1 so it goes viral.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes what 0.4 means and what drives it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the multiplier and cycle effect, decomposes k into invitations and conversion, evaluates incentives against quality, and sets guardrails and unit economics.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;At k = 0.4 each new user brings 0.4 more, so the loop is not self-sustaining, but it multiplies acquisition by 1 ÷ (1 − 0.4) = 1.67: 10,000 users acquired by other means become about 16,700 over several cycles, and the effective acquisition cost falls by 40%. That is worth having and worth improving; it is not a growth engine by itself. &quot;I'd decompose k: say 4 invitations per user at 10% conversion. Levers: invitations (make the prompt contextual, at the moment the user gets value, and give a reason to invite: sharing something the recipient will want); conversion (the invitation page and the first-use experience for the invited). Raising invitations to 5 and conversion to 12% gives k = 0.6 and a multiplier of 2.5. An incentive can raise both but may attract people who do not use the product, so I'd test it against a holdout and measure the retention and activation of referred users and the reward cost per retained user. &quot;Guardrails: spam complaints and unsubscribes, fraud (fake accounts to earn rewards), and the quality of referred users compared with other channels. Unit economics: if a reward costs $5 and a referred user is worth $30 in contribution, it pays; if referred users churn twice as fast, it may not.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Multiplier; decomposition; incentive tradeoffs; guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you launch a significant new feature to existing users? Give the plan and the success criteria.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Announce it by email and in the app.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A staged launch with a checklist and metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Targets a segment with a message, a launch tier and timeline, a readiness checklist across functions, success criteria with guardrails and rollback triggers, a holdout, and a post-launch review.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First, who it is for within the base and what problem it solves for them: say a scheduling feature for the 30% of users who book appointments weekly. The message: 'book in two taps instead of six,' with proof from the beta. The tier: a staged rollout with an announcement only after the first stage is stable (C17). &quot;Timeline. Eight weeks out: positioning, success metrics and rollout plan agreed. Six weeks: beta with 2,000 users; feedback. Four weeks: documentation, support and sales enablement, in-product messages drafted. Two weeks: a readiness review across product, engineering, design, data, support, legal and marketing, with a checklist (quality, performance, accessibility, privacy, instrumentation, help content, rollback). Launch week: 5%, then 25%, then 100% over a week, with a daily check. Two to four weeks after: a review. &quot;Success criteria, written in advance: the share of target users who use the feature at least twice in the first two weeks (primary; target 35%), repeat usage at 30 days, and impact on the core metric (bookings per user) against a 5% holdout; guardrails: support contacts per user, crash rate, and cancellations; rollback if crash rate doubles or bookings per user fall more than 2%. I'd review adoption, retention and the holdout comparison, and decide to expand, iterate or roll back.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A target and message; a staged plan; cross-functional readiness; criteria and rollback; a holdout.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customer acquisition cost has risen 40% over two quarters. Diagnose it and decide what to do.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Cut marketing spend.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by channel and checks conversion.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the effect on payback and LTV:CAC, decomposes the rise (mix, saturation, conversion, price or product changes), checks cohort quality, and decides with a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First the effect: CAC rose from $45 to $63. With contribution of $6 a month (ARPU $8, margin 75%), payback went from 7.5 to 10.5 months, and with LTV of $120 the ratio fell from 2.67 to 1.90, below the usual threshold of about 3 <code>[V: arithmetic checked; the 3:1 and 12-month benchmarks are common heuristics, not sourced]</code>. So it matters. &quot;Diagnose by decomposing CAC into spend and new customers, by channel: is spend up while volume is flat (saturation: the cheapest audiences are used up and we are buying more expensive ones), did the mix shift toward a costly channel, did conversion fall (a landing page or product change; the funnel step where it fell), did a competitor or an auction raise prices, or is attribution broken (a tracking change counts fewer customers)? I'd also check the quality: do new customers retain as well as before? A cheap but poor channel and an expensive but good one look different in CAC and the same in value. &quot;Then decide with a rule: for each channel compute LTV:CAC and payback; cut or cap channels below the threshold; shift spend to channels that pass; fix the funnel step that fell if that is the cause; and improve retention if LTV is the lever. I would not cut across the board: it may drop the good channels with the bad.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The effect on payback and ratio; decomposition; cohort quality; a channel-level rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What happens when you open an app and your feed loads?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The app asks the server for the data and shows it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Walks the path: device, name lookup, connection, server, database, response, rendering.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Says where time and failure concentrate, mentions caching and percentiles, and ties the path to what the user sees when a hop fails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll walk the path in the order it happens, and say where it can go wrong. First, the app on my phone decides what to ask for: the latest items for my account. It needs the address of the company's server, so it asks the Domain Name System, which turns a name into a numeric address. A copy of recent answers is kept on the phone or the network, so this is usually fast. Then the phone opens a secure connection to the server's front door, which is usually a load balancer that spreads requests among many servers, often behind a content delivery network that already holds common files near me. The request reaches an application server, which checks who I am, then asks other services and a database for what goes into my feed: my follows, recent posts, ranking, maybe ads. It may use a cache for popular items. It assembles a response and sends it back, and my phone draws it, loading images as I scroll. <em>Where time goes.</em> On a good network, the server and database work dominates; on a poor network, connection setup and transfer dominate; on an old phone, drawing can. So when someone says it is slow I ask which hop and at which percentile, because the median can look fine while one user in twenty waits several seconds. <em>Where it fails, and what the user sees.</em> If the name lookup fails, nothing loads. If the database is slow, the feed is slow. If one service (say ads) is down, a well-designed app shows the feed without ads and not a blank screen: graceful degradation. The thing I would check is what each hop shows the user when it fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain to a non-technical stakeholder what an API is.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's how programs talk to each other.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives a plain definition, an analogy and a product example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Adds the contract idea, versioning, and why it matters to the business (partners, speed, risk), and says one tradeoff.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> An API is a menu of requests that one piece of software offers to another. Like a restaurant menu, it says what you can ask for, in what form, and what you will get back, without you needing to know how the kitchen works. An example from a product: when a travel app shows the weather, it does not measure the weather. It sends a request to a weather company's API, &quot;forecast for this city tomorrow,&quot; and gets back a small, structured answer that the app displays. When you pay in an online shop, the shop does not handle your card; it calls a payment company's API, &quot;charge this amount,&quot; and gets back &quot;approved&quot; or &quot;declined.&quot; Why this matters for the business. First, it lets us build on others' work and lets others build on ours, which is how platforms grow. Second, an API is a <em>contract</em>: once partners build on it, we cannot change it casually, so changing it is a planned, announced process with versions. Third, it is a place where reliability and security are decided: every request is a door, and each needs a lock, a limit on how often it can be used, and monitoring. One tradeoff to know: making an API simple for partners means committing to keep it working, which limits how freely our own engineers can reshape the system behind it. So I ask what the contract is, who depends on it, and what it costs to change.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain on-device versus cloud processing for a voice assistant, including privacy and latency.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;On-device is more private and cloud is more powerful.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares latency, privacy, capability, cost and offline use.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies latency on good and poor networks, proposes a hybrid with routing and disclosure, and treats sensitive requests specially.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real system. <em>On-device processing</em> means the phone does the work itself; <em>cloud processing</em> means it sends the audio or its transcript to a server and gets the answer back. <em>Privacy.</em> On the device, the data never leaves, which is the strongest guarantee and a trust signal. In the cloud, the data is sent and handled by a server, so the company must justify what it collects, how long it keeps it, and who can see it. Users should be told which happens and given control. <em>Latency.</em> The device has no network wait, but its processor is slower than a server's. Suppose (invented) on-device compute takes 150 ms; the cloud computes in 40 ms but needs a round trip of 60 ms and 20 ms to upload on a good connection, 120 ms in all; on a poor connection with a 400 ms round trip it is 460 ms, and offline it fails. So the cloud is slightly faster when the network is good and much slower when it is not; on-device is predictable. <em>Capability.</em> The cloud can run larger models and use more data, so it handles hard requests better; the device is limited by memory and battery. Suppose easy requests (80% of traffic) are handled at about 95% accuracy on the device and hard ones at 70%, against 90% for hard requests in the cloud. <em>Cost.</em> The cloud costs the company per request (say $0.002), which at a billion requests a day is $2 million a day; the device shifts cost to the user's battery and the model's size on the phone. <em>My recommendation.</em> A hybrid: do the easy 80% on the device and send only the hard 20% to the cloud, with a short routing step. In my numbers that gives an average latency near 146 ms, accuracy near 94% (against 90% for device only and about 95% for cloud only), and a fifth of the cloud cost. I would keep sensitive categories on the device whatever their difficulty, tell users plainly what is sent, and measure the share routed to the cloud. What would change my view: on-device quality closing the gap, which would move more work to the device. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that technical-fluency prompts at Apple concern on-device processing and privacy <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product's design, numbers or data handling.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Customers say a page is slow. How do you work with engineers to find out why and what to do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ask engineering to make it faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for measurements by hop and percentile, finds the biggest contributor, and decides on a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses a latency budget, checks the slow devices and networks, considers side effects and measures the effect on users.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd turn &quot;slow&quot; into numbers before asking for any fix. I'd ask engineering for the load time at the median and the 95th percentile, split by hop: name lookup and connection, server compute, database, transfer, and rendering. I'd also ask who is slow: which devices, networks and countries, because averages hide the slow minority, and the customers who complain are often in it. Then I'd find the largest contributor at the percentile that matters. Suppose the tail is 5.2 seconds and 2.0 of it is one database query. By Amdahl's law, speeding up a hop that is 5% of the time can save at most 5%, so I would not start with the front-end polish. I'd ask the engineer what is slow about that query; a common answer is a missing index, a cheap fix. I'd also ask about image sizes (transfer) and whether any result could be cached and how stale it may be. I'd put a target in writing: a 95th-percentile load time under 3 seconds on a mid-range phone on a typical mobile connection, not &quot;faster.&quot; I'd ask what the fixes cost, what they risk (a cache can show stale data, an index slows writes slightly), and how we will roll them out with a flag and a holdback so that we can measure the effect on bounce and conversion, not just on load time. Finally I'd set a guardrail so that speed does not come at the cost of correctness, and I'd re-measure after each change. What I would not do: assign a rewrite without knowing where the time goes.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Users say that after saving a change to their profile, they sometimes see the old version. What could cause this, and what would you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's a bug; ask engineering to fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names caching and copies of data as causes, and explains staleness.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes causes (browser or app cache, server cache, read replica lag), sizes the problem, proposes read-your-own-writes and design options with tradeoffs, and sets a measure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is a classic symptom of data that exists in more than one place, so one copy is updated before the others. I'd list where copies live, because the cause depends on which one is stale. <em>Possible causes.</em> (1) <em>The client's cache:</em> the app or browser keeps the old profile and shows it until it refreshes. (2) <em>A server-side cache:</em> the application stores a copy of the profile for speed, and the write did not clear it, or the cache's lifetime has not expired. (3) <em>Read replicas:</em> to handle load, the database keeps copies that serve reads, and a copy can lag behind the main one by a short time (eventual consistency); if the page after saving reads from a lagging copy, the user sees the old data. (4) <em>A queue:</em> if the write is processed asynchronously, the change may not have been applied yet. <em>Size it.</em> Ask for the rate and timing. Suppose (invented) 200,000 profile saves a day, and 5% of users view their profile within two seconds of saving, which is about 10,000 users a day who could see stale data if replica lag is about two seconds. That is a visible, trust-damaging number: people think their change was lost, and some will save again or contact support. <em>Options.</em> (A) <em>Read-your-own-writes:</em> for a few seconds after a user writes, route their reads to the main database or the fresh cache. Cost: a small extra load on the main database (here 10,000 reads a day, negligible) and a little logic. (B) <em>Show the new data immediately on the client,</em> then reconcile (optimistic update). Cost: a brief inconsistency if the save fails, which needs a clear error. (C) <em>Shorten cache lifetimes or clear the cache on write.</em> Cost: less speed benefit. (D) <em>Accept it:</em> acceptable for data like a follower count, not for something the user just changed. <em>Decision.</em> A and B together for user-initiated changes (the user must see their own change at once), and keep the long cache lifetimes for other users' views of the profile, where a delay of seconds is harmless. Measure: the rate of &quot;I saved but see the old value&quot; support contacts and a client check of whether the displayed value matches the saved one. The lesson to say aloud: speed comes from copies, and copies can disagree, so the product decides where staleness is acceptable.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A messaging app wants to set reliability targets. How would you approach it, and what would you commit to for sending messages versus loading media?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;As reliable as possible, five nines.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sets different targets for different functions, using the cost of failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes downtime for each target, compounds dependencies, defines an SLO and error budget with a policy, and designs graceful degradation and retries with idempotency.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd begin with what a failure costs the user, because a single target for everything wastes effort. Sending a message is the core promise; if it fails, people lose trust fast. Loading a photo that arrives a moment late is an annoyance. <em>Targets and what they mean.</em> A month has about 43,834 minutes. A target of 99.95% for sending allows 0.0005 × 43,834 = about 22 minutes of failure a month. A target of 99.5% for loading media allows 219 minutes, about 3.6 hours. I would not say &quot;five nines&quot; without a price: each extra nine costs more redundancy and complexity, so I'd ask engineering what 99.95% costs against 99.9% (44 minutes) and compare it with what an extra 22 minutes of failures cost in trust. <em>Dependencies.</em> Sending depends on several services (authentication, the message store, the delivery service); in a chain, availability multiplies. Three services at 99.95%, 99.95% and 99.9% give about 99.8%, which is 88 minutes of failure, worse than the target. So either the chain must be shortened (can delivery be asynchronous?) or each part must be better. <em>Design for the failure.</em> Make sending resilient: save the message on the device, show it as &quot;sending,&quot; and retry in the background until the server confirms, with an idempotency key so that a retry never produces a duplicate message. For media, load a low-resolution preview first and fetch the full file when possible, so a slow media service degrades the experience without breaking the conversation. <em>SLO and error budget.</em> Define the objective in terms users feel: 99.95% of send attempts are accepted within two seconds, measured monthly. The budget is about 22 minutes. A policy decided in advance: if less than a quarter of the budget remains, pause risky launches and focus on reliability; if the budget is spent, only fixes ship until it recovers. <em>Measures.</em> Successful sends per attempt, the 95th and 99th percentile send time, duplicate message rate (should be zero), and how long messages sit in &quot;sending.&quot; What would change my approach: evidence that outages in one region hurt a particular market much more, which would justify regional redundancy there.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c21-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C21-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> technical · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C21 · <strong>Competency:</strong> TE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Engineering says a change requires a database migration with two hours of downtime. How do you evaluate it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do it at night and tell customers,&quot; or &quot;downtime is unacceptable.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the cost of downtime against the cost of avoiding it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Asks the ten questions, computes cost and the effect on the reliability budget and contracts, knows the expand-and-contract alternative, and plans rollback and communication.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a cost and risk decision and start with questions to engineering (FW-C21-02): why is downtime needed, what changes in the data, what is the riskiest step, and what is the rollback if it fails halfway? Is there a way to migrate without downtime? <em>Cost of the downtime.</em> Suppose (invented) the product earns $20,000 an hour at peak. A two-hour window at 03:00, when traffic is about 15% of peak, costs about 2 × $20,000 × 0.15 = $6,000 in lost sales, plus support contacts and some trust. That is small. <em>Cost of avoiding it.</em> The alternative is a staged &quot;expand and contract&quot; migration: add the new structure beside the old, write to both, copy the old data in the background, switch reads to the new, then remove the old. It avoids downtime and adds a few weeks; suppose three person-weeks at $15,000 each, $45,000, plus more moving parts and more risk of a subtle bug during the dual-write period. <em>Reliability budget and contracts.</em> At a 99.9% monthly objective the budget is 43.8 minutes; two hours is 120 minutes, about 2.7 times the budget. If customers have service agreements, check whether planned maintenance is excluded and how much notice is required; some enterprise contracts forbid planned downtime in business hours or require notice. So the cash comparison ($6,000 against $45,000) is not the whole story: the budget and contracts may force the staged approach. <em>Plan either way.</em> Rehearse the migration on a copy of production data and time it (two hours is an estimate, not a measurement). Set a go or no-go point and a rollback that is tested. Take a verified backup. Choose the quietest window and tell customers in advance with a status page. Have support ready. Define a trigger for abort (for example, if the copy is not 60% complete by the midpoint). <em>Decision.</em> If the contracts allow notice and the window is quiet, accept the planned two hours, with the rehearsal and rollback plan. If contracts or the budget forbid it, or if the data is critical and rollback is hard, fund the staged migration. What would change my mind: a rehearsal showing the migration takes six hours, which would make the staged route clearly better.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c22-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C22-01</h3>\n";
